  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 231 AM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nZonal flow aloft will strengthen today, bringing a stronger\ndownslope component and thus a bit more warming. We expect highs \nto reach 90F or more over most of the plains and I-25 Corridor. \nTo offset that, there is some mid/upper level moisture evident on \nsatellite and radar now reaching northern Utah. This will continue\nto push east today, resulting in enough for scattered virga,\nisolated high based showers, and even a lone weak storm. The main\nthreat of any light precipitation would be over the northern \nmountains this afternoon, spreading to the northern plains this \nevening, but even there coverage would be sparse. Any high based \nshowers/virga would support gusty outflow winds to 40 mph given \nincreasing environmental flow and DCAPE increasing to 800-1200 \nJ/kg. \n\nFor tonight, a cold front pushes south across the High Plains and\nthrough northeast Colorado late tonight into early Saturday\nmorning. This will lead to cooler temperatures (near normal) for\nSaturday. While moisture levels increase slightly, considerable\nuncertainty exists for afternoon and evening shower/storm coverage\ndue to thermal profiles. For the most part, we don't think we'll\nreach convective temps over the plains but still can't rule out\na few late day showers and storms spilling eastward from the\nhigher terrain due to mid level instability and moisture. \n\nSunday will likely feature a return to much above normal\ntemperatures. MOS guidance suggest highs pushing into the mid 90s\nfor metro Denver and the I-25 Urban Corridor. That may be a bit\naggressive as there is some guidance (not surprisingly) slowing \nthe redevelopment of lee troughing and erosion of low level\nmoisture. The record for Denver on the 13th is 96F set in 2018, \n1990, and 1895, but at this point it appears we'll most likely \nstay a couple degrees shy of that. With regard to convection, \nisolated showers and storms will be high based and relatively \nweak. However, if a storm or two can break the cap over the \nnortheast plains they could certainly tap into higher MLCAPE and \nbecome severe. \n\nAs discussed yesterday, the cooldown for Monday appears to be\ntempered as the next cold front/upper trough has been delayed \nslightly. However, there is still high confidence in more \nsignificant cooling for Tuesday behind a moderately strong early \n(meteorological) autumn cold front. While daily uncertainties \nexist about the degree of cooling, there is modest agreement we'll\nsettle into highs in the 70s (and 30-40% chance of 60s) for the \nplains and I-25 Corridor. A brief warmup is possible for \nWednesday before yet another front arrives Thursday. Ensembles are\nin decent agreement holding a plume of mid/upper level moisture \nand higher than normal climatological precipitable water over us.\nThus, we should have daily chances of showers and storms most of \nthe days this upcoming week.",
